{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Remember</b> — This does not speak of the exile of Titus, as many thought, for afterward he mentions Daughter Babylon. Furthermore: wicked Titus was not of the seed of Edom, for the people of Rome are Greeks by descent. Rather, just as Edom had been under Israel's hand, they rejoiced in Israel's downfall, and thus it is written in the book of Obadiah: \"you too shall be cut off.\" And the taunt with which the Edomites taunted Israel on the day Jerusalem was captured and the Temple burned by the Chaldeans was harder for Israel than the calamity itself. And so he says: \"Do not enlarge your mouth.\" And this is the meaning of \"Remember\": those who said to the Babylonians, \"Strip bare, strip bare, even to her foundations,\" concerning Jerusalem. Now ערו is from the root of פתהן יערה, and it should have been on the pattern of כלו מעשיכם, with the resh lacking a dagesh as is the rule. But because it is at the end of a verse, the word is milra, like כלו in כלו בעשן כלו. And since the accent in the last word ערו falls on the first letter, therefore the first ערו as well reverts to milra."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 137, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:00.520726567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}